--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 3476 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: in almost every case you should redact all but the last four digits before sending a scanned document unless the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN and gives you a secure way to transmit it.

Why: the full SSN is extremely sensitive PII. Many organizations accept just the last four digits for verification. Sending a full SSN over ordinary email or file sharing increases your risk of identity theft.

What to do (practical steps)

1) Confirm whether the recipient actually needs the full SSN
- Ask them directly: “Do you need the full SSN, or will the last four digits suffice?”  
- If they require the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (secure portal, encrypted transfer) and their data handling policy.

2) If full SSN is NOT required — redact everything except the last four
- Mask the first five digits so only the last four show (for example: ***-**-3456). Do not just scribble lightly or cover with whiteout that can be reversed.

3) Redaction methods (choose based on file type and tools)

PDF (recommended if available)
- Use a proper Redact tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions). Then use “Remove Hidden Information”/“Sanitize” and save as a new file. This truly removes underlying text and metadata.
- If you don’t have Acrobat, overlay an opaque black box and then “Print to PDF” (or Export/Flatten) to make the blackout permanent.

Scanned image or photo
- Edit the image in an editor (Paint, Preview, Photoshop) and draw a completely opaque black box over the SSN. Save as a new file (JPEG/PNG/PDF).
- Or redact on a physical copy with a thick permanent marker or black tape, then scan the redacted copy. (If you redact AFTER scanning, ensure you flatten/export so the redaction can’t be removed.)

Mac Preview quick method
- Draw a black rectangle over the SSN, then File → Print → PDF → Save as PDF to flatten.

Windows without editor
- Open the scan in an image editor, black out the area, then save or “Print to PDF” to flatten.

4) Verify your redaction
- Try to select/copy the SSN from the resulting PDF — if you can select text that’s a sign it wasn’t removed.
- Search the PDF for the digits; if they appear, redaction failed.
- Inspect and remove metadata/hidden text (Acrobat’s “Sanitize” or “Remove hidden information”). Don’t upload to untrusted online redaction sites.

5) Securely transmit the redacted file
- Best: upload to the recipient’s secure portal or use an encrypted file transfer (SFTP, SharePoint/OneDrive/Box with restricted access, or a vendor portal).
- If you must email: password-protect the PDF (use strong password/encryption), send the password by a separate channel (phone call or SMS).
- Consider links that expire and require authentication.

6) Redact other sensitive fields as appropriate
- Date of birth, driver’s license, account numbers, or addresses if not needed — only share what’s required.

Other notes
- Even if you plan to share only the last four digits, avoid including the whole SSN elsewhere (including in chat or unencrypted email).  
- If an organization requests full SSN and won’t provide a secure method, ask for an alternative verification method.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether the last four are sufficient and/or requesting a secure upload link.
- Give step-by-step redaction instructions tailored to your OS (Windows/Mac/iPhone/Android) or the software you have. Which would you like?

