Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — you should redact your SSN unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number and gives a secure way to send it. If you must send anything, prefer sending only the last four digits and use a secure channel.

What to do (quick checklist)
- If possible, send only the last four digits (most organizations accept this for ID confirmation).
- If the full SSN is required, use a secure method (secure portal, encrypted email, or a password-protected file; never send a full SSN over plain email or SMS).
- Properly redact the SSN in the file (use a true redaction tool — not just a black marker drawn on a scanned copy).
- Verify the redaction actually removed the underlying text and metadata before sending.
- When in doubt, ask the recipient how they want the SSN transmitted and whether last four will suffice.

How to redact safely (step-by-step)
1. Work with the original digital file if you can (PDF, Word, image). If you only have a paper version, scan after redaction.
2. Use a proper redaction tool:
   - PDFs: Adobe Acrobat Pro (Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply), or other PDF editors that offer a “redact” feature that removes underlying text/metadata.
   - Mac: Preview’s black box is not guaranteed — better to use a PDF redaction tool or export text as an image and edit, then re-save.
   - Images: Use an image editor to permanently paint over the SSN area and save as a new file (flatten the image).
3. Remove hidden text and metadata: use the “Sanitize” / “Remove hidden information” feature in your PDF editor.
4. Save as a new file and verify:
   - Open the saved file and try selecting or searching the redacted area to confirm the numbers are gone.
   - Check file properties/metadata to make sure nothing sensitive remains.
5. If sending a PDF, consider adding password protection and share the password separately (call or different channel).

What NOT to do
- Don’t just draw a black box on a paper copy and then scan — some scanners/optimizers may retain underlying text or the original file might still exist.
- Don’t send the full SSN over unencrypted email, SMS, or chat.
- Don’t upload to an unknown file-sharing service without checking its security and retention policy.

Secure sending options
- Recipient’s secure portal or upload form (best).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or enterprise secure file transfer.
- Password-protected PDF; send the password by phone or different channel.
- If the recipient insists on email, ask them to confirm whether TLS is sufficient and whether they have a secure method to receive sensitive data.

A quick message you can send the recipient
- “Do you need my full SSN, or will the last 4 digits be sufficient? If you need the full SSN, please provide a secure upload method or confirm how you want it transmitted.”

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email asking the recipient whether last four digits are acceptable or requesting secure upload instructions.
- Walk you through redacting a specific file (tell me what type of file — PDF, image, Word — and I’ll give concrete steps).

