Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — avoid sending the full SSN in a scanned document unless the recipient expressly requires it and provides a secure way to receive it. Limit what you share (usually the last 4 digits) and if you must send the full number, use a secure channel and use a true redaction method so the number cannot be recovered.

Practical steps and checklist

1. Confirm what the form actually requires
- Check the form wording: many forms only need the last 4 digits for ID purposes. If it only asks for the last four, send only that.
- If the form explicitly requires the full SSN, ask the organization for a secure upload method before sending.

2. If you will redact the SSN in a scan (recommended)
- Best practice: remove or permanently redact all but the last 4 digits (if last 4 are acceptable).
- Use a proper redaction tool (preferred):
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: use the Redact tool — it removes underlying text and metadata.
  - PDF-XChange Editor, PDFpen, or other PDF editors that offer secure redaction.
  - After redaction, verify you cannot select/copy the redacted text.
- Quick physical method:
  - Print the document, permanently black out the SSN with an opaque permanent marker, then rescan or photograph the page. Verify the blacked-out area cannot be read in the scan.
  - Or cover the SSN with a plain strip of paper when taking the photo.
- If editing an image:
  - Open in an image editor, paint over the SSN pixels, flatten and save as a new file, then remove image metadata (EXIF).
  - Don’t rely on low-quality “blurring” or simply placing a black box over text in an editable PDF — those can sometimes be reversed if the underlying text remains.

3. Remove metadata
- Strip EXIF from photos and metadata from PDFs. Tools: ExifTool, “Remove Properties and Personal Information” on Windows, or export a flattened PDF in Acrobat.

4. Secure transmission
- Preferred: use the recipient’s secure portal (upload form) or an encrypted file transfer.
- If you must email:
  - Send a password-protected PDF (use Acrobat or zip the file with a strong password) and give the password by a separate channel (phone or SMS).
  - Use encrypted email (S/MIME/PGP) if available.
- Always confirm recipient email/portal is legitimate before sending.

5. Verify after redaction
- Open the final file and try to copy/select the redacted text or view metadata to ensure the SSN cannot be recovered.

6. Minimize retention
- Don’t keep unnecessary copies. Once delivered and accepted, delete local copies if you don’t need them.

