Practical guidance for sending a scanned form safely

1) Confirm whether the full SSN is required
- Ask the requester: “Do you need the full SSN or will the last four digits suffice?” and ask for a secure upload option if they require the full number.
- Many employers/government agencies accept last four; some tax or identity-verification processes do require full SSN.

2) If full SSN is not required
- Redact everything except the last four digits (for example: XXX-XX-1234) before scanning or sending.

3) How to redact properly (physical and digital)
- Physical paper:
  - Use a thick permanent black marker to fully obscure the digits, then photocopy or scan the marked page. Verify the obscured digits cannot be read when you zoom or adjust brightness/contrast.
  - Alternatively, cut out the SSN portion, then scan the remaining page.
- Digital/PDF:
  - Use a proper PDF redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact). That permanently removes the redacted text from the file and metadata.
  - If you don’t have redaction tools: convert the page to an image (flatten), use an image editor to paint over the digits at the pixel level, save, then create a new PDF from that image. Do NOT just draw a black rectangle on top of selectable text without flattening or redaction—underlying text or OCR layers can still expose the number.
- After redaction, open the final file and try to copy/search to confirm the SSN text is gone.

4) Remove metadata and hidden data
- Before sending, remove metadata/hidden text (PDF properties, EXIF on images, OCR layers). Many PDF editors have a “remove hidden information” or “sanitize” feature.
- On Windows/macOS you can also "print to PDF" from the redacted file to create a fresh file without metadata.

5) Use a secure delivery method
- Prefer secure upload portals (employer/hr portals, government secure forms). These are the safest.
- If you must email, do one of:
  - Send a password-protected PDF (use a strong password) and share the password separately (text/call).
  - Use an encrypted file transfer/secure link with expiration (SFTP, secure file-share with link expiry, vendor secure upload).
- Avoid sending a full SSN in unencrypted plain email whenever possible. Note: TLS email encryption protects in transit only when both servers support it; it’s not the same as end-to-end encryption.

6) Keep minimal copies and delete when done
- Keep a copy only if necessary. Remove scanned copies from devices and cloud storage when no longer needed.
- If you must store them, use encrypted storage.

7) If the recipient insists they need the full SSN
- Ask for their secure submission instructions (secure portal, encrypted upload).
- If they cannot provide a secure method, ask if an in-person drop-off or phone confirmation is possible.

