- Verify redaction worked: try selecting text, copy/paste, or open the PDF in a text editor — redacted text should not be recoverable.

Secure sending options
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal (preferred for banks, HR, tax/medical portals).  
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) if both parties support it.  
- Password-protected PDF (AES-256) can add protection — send the password by a separate channel (phone or SMS is OK for the password). Note: password-protection is not as strong as end-to-end secure portal but better than plain email.  
- Secure file transfer services that support end-to-end encryption (ShareFile, OneDrive “secure link” with expiry and authentication, Box, Dropbox with password/expiration).  
- Avoid sending an SSN in plain email, SMS, or unsecured messaging apps.

Other practical tips
- Don’t include SSN in filenames or in the email subject line. Use a neutral filename (e.g., ID_document.pdf).  
- Limit who receives it. Verify the recipient’s email and identity before sending.  
- Keep a copy of the securely sent version and securely delete any unsecured copies.  
- If the request is from a company, ask whether they have a secure upload option — many do for handling SSNs.  
- For regulated documents: follow specific guidance (e.g., employer/payroll/IRS may require full SSN and provide a secure method).

