Short answer: Yes — avoid sending the full SSN in a plain scanned file or regular email unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure method. In most cases you should redact (mask) everything except the final 4 digits — and only share those if the form/recipient accepts last‑4 verification.

A few important notes before steps
- I won’t repeat the full number you typed back to you. But I did notice the “last four” you wrote doesn’t match the final four digits of the full SSN you typed — please double‑check which set you actually intend to share.
- Treat the full SSN as highly sensitive. Use data‑minimization: only share what’s necessary.

How to redact safely (general workflow)
1. Make a copy of the document or scan — never edit the only original.
2. If you only need to show last 4 digits, cover or permanently remove all other digits. Don’t just “hide” them visually without applying a true redaction (see below).
3. Use a proper redaction tool so the removed text is irrecoverable (not just covered by a black box on top of text).
4. Verify the result by opening the redacted file and searching or trying to select/copy the area to confirm the full SSN isn’t recoverable or present in hidden text/OCR.
5. Remove metadata and embedded text/OCR (see below).
6. Send the redacted copy only via a secure channel (portal, encrypted email, passworded file + separate password, or secure file transfer).

How to perform redaction on common platforms
- Adobe Acrobat Pro (best/most reliable):
  - Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction (select the SSN digits) → Apply Redactions → Save as a new file.
  - Acrobat’s redaction removes underlying text and metadata when you choose to remove hidden information.
- Mac Preview (less ideal because it can leave underlying text):
  - Best approach: draw black boxes on a copy, then Export/Print to PDF as an image (Print → Save as PDF) so text is flattened. Still verify by trying to copy/select text.
- Microsoft Word / Windows:
  - Paste the scan into Word or open PDF in a PDF editor that supports true redaction (e.g., PDF-XChange, Nitro, Acrobat). Use their redaction tool. If you must place a black box, convert/print to a new PDF to flatten, then verify.
- Mobile (iOS/Android):
  - Use Adobe Fill & Sign to fill forms and add blackout annotations, then save/export. Prefer apps that explicitly “flatten” or “apply” annotations. Verify by attempting to select/copy text or open the file on a computer.
- If you can’t do true redaction, print, blackout with a permanent marker, then re‑scan. This physical method is effective if you then confirm the re‑scan does not contain visible or OCR text.

Remove embedded data / OCR / metadata
- After redaction, ensure OCR text behind the image is removed. Search the PDF for the SSN digits.
- Strip metadata (EXIF/IPTC) from images and PDFs. Tools: ExifTool, ImageOptim, or the “Remove hidden information” feature in Acrobat.
- Rename the file to avoid including the SSN in the filename.

Secure transmission options
- Best: Upload to the recipient’s secure portal (bank/HR/government portals often provide this). Confirm the portal’s URL is legitimate.
- Encrypted email: S/MIME or PGP if both parties support it.
- Password‑protected PDF: Use strong password; send the password via a different channel (call or SMS).
- Secure file transfer services (OneDrive/SharePoint with restricted link, Dropbox with expiring link and password, or a dedicated secure file transfer tool).
- Physical mail or in‑person delivery, if acceptable.

If the recipient is requesting the full SSN
- Ask them to provide a secure upload link or an alternative secure channel before you send the full SSN.
- If they insist on email, ask for their organization’s encryption policy or a secure fax number.

Checklist before sending
- Did you redact everything except the minimal required digits?
- Did you use a proper redaction tool or physically redact and re‑scan?
- Did you remove metadata and verify OCR isn’t exposing the number?
- Is the transmission method secure (portal/encryption/password + separate channel)?
- Is the filename free of the SSN?

Would you like help with any of the following?
- Step‑by‑step redaction instructions for a specific device or app (Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android).
- Drafting a message to the recipient asking for a secure upload method.
- Verifying whether the form truly requires the full SSN (you can paste the form text, I can help interpret).

Also confirm whether you intended the final four digits you listed (they didn’t match the ending of the full SSN you typed) so I can tailor advice (e.g., which digits to display).

