  - Never put the SSN in the email subject or filename.

How to redact safely

- Physical paper:
  - Use a permanent black marker over the digits you want to hide, then photocopy the page (marker ink can sometimes be read through on scans if you only scan). Scan the photocopy.
  - Or physically cut/crop out the SSN area, then scan.
- Digital scan / PDF:
  - Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat’s Redact tool) that removes the underlying text and metadata.
  - If you don’t have Acrobat: open image in an editor, draw a solid black box over the digits, then “print to PDF” or export as a flattened image/PDF (this prevents the original text layer from being recoverable).
  - Don’t rely on blur or low-opacity boxes — these can sometimes be reversed or OCRed.
- Images (phone photos):
  - Take the photo with the SSN covered, or edit and permanently flatten the image after blacking out.
  - Remove metadata/EXIF (many phones or apps offer “remove location/metadata” on export).

Other security steps

- Remove metadata from PDFs/images before sending.
- Avoid sending the SSN in a filename, subject line, or preview text.
- If you must send the full SSN, confirm the recipient’s privacy/security practices and retain proof of secure upload or encryption.
- Consider mailing a physical copy via tracked/registered mail if digital security cannot be assured.

