Short answer: yes — treat the full SSN as highly sensitive and only share the full number when absolutely required and only over a secure channel. In most cases you should redact everything except the minimum the recipient actually needs (often just the last four digits).

Practical guidance and checklist

1. Confirm what the recipient actually needs
- Ask whether they need the full SSN on the scan or if the last four digits will suffice. If they require the full SSN, request a secure upload portal or an encrypted transmission method before sending.

2. Redact everything you don’t need to show
- If the recipient only needs the last four digits, redact the other five digits and any other instances of the full SSN on the document.
- Common redaction formats: XXX-XX-1234 or simply 1234 (use whatever the recipient requests).

3. Use proper, permanent redaction (don’t just hide it visually)
- Best: use a PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool) which removes the underlying text/pixels permanently.
- If you only black out text visually (e.g., draw a black box in Word or Paint), the underlying text may still be recoverable. After blacking out, flatten and export to a new PDF and verify the text is gone.
- For images: edit in an image editor, fill with solid black, then save and convert to PDF — then verify there’s no readable/hidden text.

4. Remove hidden data and OCR/text layers
- PDFs can contain an invisible text layer from OCR. After redaction, check that selecting/copying text doesn’t reveal the redacted SSN. Use “Remove hidden information” or metadata removal tools where available.

5. Use a secure transmission method
- Preferred: the recipient’s secure portal, encrypted email, SFTP, or a dedicated secure file-share.
- If you must email, encrypt the attachment or use a password-protected PDF and send the password separately (phone or different channel).
- Avoid uploading documents with SSNs to public cloud links without password protection.

6. Verify after redaction
- Open the redacted file and try to select/copy the redacted area; it should not be recoverable.
- Keep a copy of the original for your records, but store it securely and delete local copies you no longer need.

7. Compliance considerations
- Follow any industry-specific rules (HIPAA, GLBA, employer requirements). Some organizations won’t accept scans with redactions — confirm their policy.

