Short answer: Yes — redact the full SSN unless the requester explicitly needs the whole number and gives a secure way to send it. Keep only what's required (usually just the last four digits) and use proper redaction tools and secure transfer.

What to do, step-by-step
1. Confirm requirement
 - Ask the recipient whether they actually need the full SSN or if last four digits suffice (e.g., "XXX-XX-3456" or just "3456").
 - If they say they need the full SSN, request a secure upload method (encrypted portal, secure file transfer, or encrypted email) before sending.

2. If only last four are needed
 - Provide only the last four (3456) or show masked form: XXX-XX-3456 or 000-00-3456 → XXX-XX-3456.
 - Do not include the full 000-00-0123 in the file or email.

3. If you must send a scan with redacted SSN (recommended approach)
 - Use a proper redaction tool (best): Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool — mark the SSN and apply redaction, then Save As a new file. This removes text and metadata.
 - If you don’t have Acrobat Pro: convert the page to an image (export page to PNG/JPEG or print-to-PDF as an image), edit the image to remove the SSN (crop or paint over), then save as a new PDF. This rasterizes the content so underlying text cannot be recovered.
 - Avoid just drawing a black box in a PDF viewer that leaves the underlying text accessible. Some “markup” tools only cover the text visually but keep it searchable/accessible.
 - If you physically redact with a marker, re-scan the redacted page and verify the text is unreadable in the scan. Note: some markers can be reversed by image processing; software redaction is safer.

4. Remove hidden data
 - After redaction, remove metadata, comments, hidden layers, and form fields (Acrobat has “Sanitize Document” / remove hidden information). Save as a new file.

5. Send securely
 - Use the requester’s secure portal if available.
 - If emailing, encrypt the message or attach a password-protected file (use a strong password and share the password by phone or separate channel).
 - Limit access to cloud links (expiration date, require sign-in, restrict to the recipient).

6. Extra caution about last four
 - Last four digits are less sensitive but still used for identity verification. Only share them when necessary.

Sample message you can send to the requester
"Before I send the document, do you need my full SSN or will the last four digits be sufficient? For privacy, I prefer to send the full SSN only through a secure upload/portal or via encrypted email."

If you want, I can:
 - Draft that email for you to send.
 - Help redact a specific scanned file if you tell me what tool you have (Acrobat, Preview, Windows, etc.).
 - Search your inbox for a secure upload link from the recipient (if you want me to look, tell me what to search for).

