Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: usually you should NOT send an unredacted full SSN unless the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure method to receive it. If they only need the last four digits, send only the last four and redact the rest.

A few quick points and practical steps:

1) Clarify what the recipient actually needs
- Ask the organization whether they need the full SSN or just the last four. Many processes accept only the last four. If they require the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or encrypted transfer instructions.

2) If you only need to send the last four
- Redact or mask the other digits so only the last four remain visible.
- Do not include the SSN anywhere else (file name, subject line, email body, or scan metadata).

3) If you must send the full SSN
- Use a secure channel: a designated secure portal/encrypted upload, SFTP, or an encrypted email attachment (PDF encrypted with a strong password; share the password separately, e.g., by phone or SMS).
- Avoid sending full SSN in plain email or as an unprotected image.

4) How to redact properly (important)
- Use a true redaction tool that removes underlying text and OCR layers (not just drawing a black box over text).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply. Then Sanitize Document to clear metadata.
  - PDF redaction apps (PDFpen, Nitro, etc.) offer similar secure redaction.
- For scanned images: open the image/PDF in an image editor or redaction-capable PDF tool, draw a black box and then flatten/export so the pixels covering the SSN are permanently applied. Verify the digits are not recoverable by selecting text or inspecting metadata.
- On mobile: avoid uploading to untrusted third-party apps. If you must, redact after scanning and then export a flattened PDF.
- After redaction, verify by opening the file and trying to select or copy the area—if you can copy the digits, redaction is not complete.

5) Remove metadata and sensitive traces
- Remove SSN from file name and email subject.
- Sanitize metadata (many PDF tools have a “Remove hidden information” or “Sanitize” feature).
- If the original document had an OCR layer, make sure the redaction removes it or re-create the PDF from an image (flattened).

6) Delivery best practices
- Send via the recipient’s secure portal whenever possible.
- If using email, attach a password-protected PDF and send the password separately (phone or SMS).
- Keep a short record of what you sent, when, and to whom.
- Confirm receipt and that they can access the file.

7) If you suspect accidental exposure
- Contact the recipient and request deletion/secure disposal.
- Consider a fraud alert or credit freeze and monitor credit reports if exposure was significant.

One last note: I noticed the full SSN you typed and the “last four” you typed don’t match. Do you want to confirm which is correct? If you’d like, tell me which device/software you plan to use (Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android, Adobe Acrobat, etc.) and I can give step-by-step redaction and secure-send instructions for that setup.

