Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share the full SSN if the form explicitly requires it and you must submit it to a trusted, secure recipient. Otherwise share only what's requested (often just the last four digits). Do not send the full number over regular email or in an unencrypted scan.

Guidance and actionable steps

1) Confirm what the form actually requires
- Check the form instructions: does it ask for the full SSN or just the last four? If it asks for the full SSN, confirm whether you can enter it into the form directly (preferred) or must upload a copy.
- If you can, enter only the required digits (for example, last four = 3456) rather than uploading a scan containing the whole number.

2) If you must upload a copy that contains your SSN, prefer alternatives to sending the full SSN
- Ask the receiver if a secure upload portal or in-person verification is available.
- If they accept a redacted copy, redact everything except the minimum required (usually last four).

3) How to redact correctly (don’t just visually hide it)
- Use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” tool, official PDF redaction tools) which removes the underlying text and metadata.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: open the document, place a solid black box over the SSN, then “Flatten” or “Print to PDF” (Print → Save as PDF). This converts annotations to image content and removes selectable/hidden text.
- Verify by trying to copy/paste the redacted area or searching the PDF for the SSN — it must not appear.
- For images/photos: crop out the SSN or use an image editor to permanently black it out and save as a new file (do not rely on reversible edits).

4) Remove metadata
- After redaction, remove metadata/EXIF (Photos on phones, PDFs often contain hidden data). Use “Save As” or an option to remove document properties, or a metadata removal tool.

5) Secure the file for transmission
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal whenever possible.
- If you must send via email: password-protect the file (PDF password or encrypted ZIP) and send the password separately (text or call).
- Use strong passwords and, if available, end-to-end encrypted email or file-transfer services.
- Avoid sending the password in the same email.

6) Filenames and subject lines
- Don’t include the SSN in the filename or email subject. Use something neutral like “Requested document — [Your Name].pdf”.

7) Keep records and limit copies
- Keep a note of what you sent, when, and to whom. Ask the recipient to confirm secure deletion if you provided a copy with the full SSN.
- Delete temporary files you created for redaction.

8) If you’re unsure or worried about fraud
- Ask the requester to explain why the full SSN is needed and whether a last-4 or other ID will suffice.
- If the request is unexpected, verify legitimacy (call a known number for the organization, not a number in the email).

If you tell me what device or software you’re using (Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android) and whether you have Adobe Acrobat or only built-in tools, I can give step‑by‑step redaction and secure-send instructions.

