Quick note — you wrote two different values (one full SSN-like string and a different “last four”). I won’t repeat the full number here — can you confirm which is correct if you want me to reference it?

Key guidance
- Don’t send the full SSN unless absolutely required. Only share the full number when the recipient explicitly requires it (e.g., certain payroll/tax or government verifications) and you’ve confirmed a secure upload channel.
- In most cases the last 4 digits are sufficient for ID/verification. Ask the requester first: “Can you accept only the last 4 digits?” If they say yes, redact everything except the last 4.
- Treat the SSN as highly sensitive PII — use secure transmission, remove copies afterward, and redact properly so the full number cannot be recovered.

How to redact safely
- Best practice: redact at the source (create a copy and redact the copy). Don’t try to redact the original document you need to keep.
- For PDFs: use a proper PDF redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat’s Redact feature or another reputable redaction feature). Redaction tools remove text/data permanently; drawing a black box over the text (without a true redact operation) can leave the underlying text searchable/recoverable.
- For images/photographs: overwrite the digits at the pixel level (paint over them, then flatten and save as a new file format like JPEG/PNG). After editing, open the file and zoom in to confirm the numbers are unrecoverable.
- For physical documents: make a photocopy, redact the copy with an opaque marker, then scan the redacted copy. Confirm the scanned image doesn’t show the original beneath the marker.
- Remove metadata: strip EXIF or PDF metadata that might contain hidden data. Some image/PDF editors or privacy tools can remove metadata.

Secure transmission
- Use the requester’s secure portal or an encrypted upload link if available (this is the preferred route).
- If you must email, send a password‑protected PDF (strong password) and send the password via a separate channel (text or phone).
- Avoid sending sensitive attachments over unsecured public Wi‑Fi or attaching full SSNs to unencrypted mail.

After sending
- Ask the recipient to confirm receipt and to delete any copies they don’t need.
- Delete local copies you no longer need and empty your trash/recycle bin. If you used a cloud service, remove copies there too and confirm deletion if possible.

Legal/identity-theft notes
- SSNs are very sensitive and regularly used for identity theft. Limit exposure to the minimum necessary.
- Employers, banks, and government agencies commonly request full SSNs for payroll/tax or benefits; other parties should be questioned about necessity and secure handling.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email/ message asking the recipient whether the last 4 digits are sufficient and requesting a secure upload link.
- Walk you step‑by‑step through redacting a particular file type (PDF, photo) — tell me which you have.
- Confirm which number is correct (if you want me to refer to your last four) — but don’t paste the full SSN back here for safety.

