--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 3157 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — you should redact everything except the minimum the recipient actually needs (usually only the last four digits). Never include the full SSN in an unencrypted email or on an unprotected scan unless the recipient specifically requires it and provides a secure way to send it.

What to do (quick checklist)
- Ask whether the recipient truly needs the full SSN. If they only need verification, give only the last 4 digits.
- If you must send any SSN, use a secure channel: a trusted secure upload portal, encrypted email, SFTP, or deliver in person. Do not send full SSN over plain email or unencrypted cloud links.
- Redact the SSN properly — use a redaction tool that removes the underlying text, not just covers it visually.
- Remove identifying info from filename (don’t name file with SSN).
- Password-protect the PDF and share the password by a separate channel (e.g., text or phone).
- Remove embedded metadata / hidden text / OCR text from the file before sending.

How to redact safely (practical options)
- Best (if available): Use a dedicated PDF redaction feature (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply). That permanently removes the redacted text from the file.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: convert the page to a flat image (scan or take a photo), then draw a solid black box over the SSN and save as PDF/image. IMPORTANT: after annotating, “flatten” or Print→Save as PDF so the redaction becomes part of the image; otherwise selectable hidden text or layers might remain.
- Avoid “white-out” or drawing black boxes in an office program without flattening — the original text can still be recovered.
- After redaction/flattening, run OCR only if you want searchable text — but don’t OCR before redaction, because OCR can preserve the original text. Better: redact first, then OCR if necessary.

Check metadata and hidden content
- Remove metadata: some PDF tools have “Remove hidden information” or “Sanitize document” features. If not available, convert to image and recreate the PDF (that strips most metadata).
- Verify by trying to select/copy the redacted area — you should not be able to copy the redacted digits.

Secure transmission tips
- Use the recipient’s secure portal or an encrypted file transfer. Many banks, employers, government agencies offer secure upload links.
- If you must email, attach the password-protected PDF and send the password separately (call or SMS).
- Don’t include SSN in the email body or subject line.
- Avoid public Wi‑Fi when uploading or sending sensitive files.

Other considerations
- Keep only one copy of the scan where needed, and delete extraneous copies from devices/clouds when finished.
- Name the file without personal identifiers (e.g., “ID_document.pdf” not “SSN_000-00-0123.pdf”).
- If the request comes from someone unfamiliar, verify the request by contacting the organization via a verified phone number.

If you tell me which device or software you’re using (phone: iPhone or Android, Windows, Mac; and which apps like Adobe, Preview, Microsoft Office), I can give step‑by‑step instructions for redacting and saving the file securely.

