Short answer: you should avoid sending the full SSN unless absolutely required. Wherever possible send only the last four digits. If you must send a scan with the full SSN, redact (permanently remove) everything you don’t need, and transmit the file over a secure channel.

Important safety points (do NOT repeat the full number in messages, subject lines, or filenames)
- Only share the full SSN if the recipient explicitly requires it and you’ve verified their identity and why it’s needed.
- Prefer sharing just the last four digits whenever that suffices.
- Don’t include the SSN in the email subject, filename, or body text unless necessary.
- Use secure transmission (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file with the password sent separately by phone/SMS).

How to redact a scanned document safely
- Best (recommended): Use a proper PDF redaction tool that permanently removes text/characters from the file (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools > Redact > Mark for Redaction (select the SSN) > Apply Redactions > Save As a new file.
- If you only have an image or a simple PDF:
  - Convert the file to an image (or take a photo), use an image editor to draw a solid black box over the SSN, then save/export as a new, flattened image or PDF. Confirm you cannot select or copy the hidden text.
  - If you overlay a box in a PDF editor that leaves the original text layer intact, the redaction is not safe (text can be recovered). After overlay, print to PDF or “export as PDF” to flatten; then test by trying to select the SSN text—if selectable, it’s not redacted.
- Avoid using a black marker on a paper original and scanning that copy unless you test the scanned result. Ink may show through or be partially recoverable; better to redact digitally.
- Remove metadata/EXIF from images and PDFs before sending (many tools and websites can strip metadata).

Transmission recommendations
- Use the recipient’s secure upload portal (best).
- If using email, use encrypted email or send a password‑protected PDF (use a strong password and share that password by phone or other separate channel).
- Avoid sending the password in the same email as the attachment.

Checklist before sending
1. Confirm the recipient actually needs the full SSN (ask if last four will do).
2. Create a redacted copy using a redaction tool or by flattening a blacked-out image.
3. Remove or strip metadata from the file.
4. Use a secure delivery method (portal, encrypted email, or passworded PDF + separate password).
5. Save the redacted copy and securely delete any copies you don’t need (and remove from email drafts/sent if appropriate).

Legal/privacy note
- Follow relevant rules that apply to you (employer, state/federal, HIPAA for health contexts, GDPR if in EU, etc.). If this is for a government, tax, or financial institution, check their submission instructions — they often provide secure upload options.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message asking the recipient whether the last four digits will suffice.
- Walk you step‑by‑step through redacting with a specific tool (Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, Microsoft Edge, or a phone app). Tell me which tool or device you’re using and I’ll give precise steps.

