Short answer
- Only give the full SSN when absolutely required. Often organizations only need the last four digits. Ask the requester whether the last four will suffice.
- If you must send the full SSN, use a truly secure transmission method and remove any recoverable copies or metadata from the file before sending.
- If you’re only asked for identification, send just the last four (3456) instead of the whole number.

What to ask the recipient before sending
- Do you actually need the full SSN, or will the last four digits suffice?
- How do you prefer to receive it securely? (secure portal, encrypted email, phone, fax)
- How long will you retain/delete the file and how will it be protected?

How to redact properly (important)
- Do NOT just draw a black box annotation over the SSN in a PDF or Word doc. Simple annotations or overlays can often be removed or the underlying text recovered.
- Use a proper redaction tool that removes the underlying content (not just hides it). Or permanently remove the SSN from the source and save a new file.
- If you can’t use a redaction tool:
  - Convert the page to a flattened image (scan or export the page as an image), then edit the image to remove the SSN (paint it out), save as a new image/PDF. That eliminates hidden text layers.
  - After editing, save/export to a new PDF and verify there’s no selectable/searchable text containing the SSN (no OCR text layer).

Platform-specific quick methods
- Adobe Acrobat Pro (recommended): Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → select SSN → Apply Redactions → Save As a new file. This removes the text permanently.
- Mac Preview: Markup black boxes then File → Export as PDF. Because Preview may not fully redact text layers, better to Print → Save as PDF (this flattens) and then confirm you cannot select or search the SSN.
- Windows without Acrobat: Open scanned page in Paint, paint over SSN, Save As PNG/JPG, then Print to PDF (this flattens). Confirm no selectable text remains.
- Mobile: Use a scanning app that supports redaction/flattening (Adobe Scan, Microsoft Lens) and export a flattened PDF.
- If you used OCR when scanning, make sure to remove OCR/text layer or re-scan without OCR.

Metadata and hidden data
- Remove document metadata and any hidden text layers. In Acrobat: File → Properties → remove metadata; use “Sanitize Document” if available.
- If you used Word/Office: Inspect Document → Remove Hidden Data before exporting to PDF.

Secure transmission options
- Best: Secure upload portal provided by the recipient (e.g., HR portal, secure form) — use that when available.
- Encrypted email: Use S/MIME or PGP if you and the recipient both support it.
- Password-protected PDF: Encrypt with a strong password (AES-256) and send the password to the recipient via a different channel (phone or SMS). Note: password-protection is decent but not as strong as portal/encrypted email.
- Secure file share: Share a link from OneDrive/Google Drive/Dropbox with link expiration, access restricted to the recipient, and a password if possible.
- Phone: Read the SSN over the phone to a verified recipient if they accept it that way.
- Fax: Traditional but can be acceptable for some organizations.

After sending
- Ask for confirmation of receipt and destruction policy (when they will delete or how they will store it).
- Keep a record of when you sent it and by which secure method.
- Delete local/temporary copies you no longer need; shred paper copies.

When it’s safe to send only last four
- Many identity checks, support requests, and forms accept only the last four digits. If the form or recipient doesn’t explicitly require the full SSN, give just the last four (3456) and a photo ID if needed.

