Short answer: usually you should redact the full SSN unless the recipient explicitly requires the complete number and the transmission method is secure. Sharing only the last 4 digits (e.g., XXX-XX-3456) is commonly acceptable — but confirm with the organization requesting the scan.

Detailed, practical guidance

1) Decide whether the full SSN is actually required
- Ask the requester: do they accept the last four only or a masked SSN? If their form explicitly asks for the full SSN and they are the official recipient (e.g., government agency, employer) you may need to provide the full number — but use a secure channel (see below).
- If the full SSN is NOT required, mask it (example format: XXX-XX-3456) and do not send the full number.

2) How to redact correctly (digital redaction — preferred)
- Best method: use a proper redaction tool that permanently removes the content and flattens the file.
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save As new file.
  - The redaction tool removes both visible text and any hidden/selectable text layers.
- If you don’t have Acrobat:
  - Convert the document to an image (scan to PNG/JPG), edit the image to replace the SSN with a solid box (not a semi-transparent marker), then save and re-create a PDF from that image (this flattens the content).
  - Alternatively, use a PDF editor that supports permanent redaction or “print to PDF” after covering the text to flatten it.
- IMPORTANT: After redaction, test the file:
  - Try selecting or searching the PDF for the redacted digits — if they’re still selectable/searchable, the redaction wasn’t done properly.
  - Zoom, increase contrast or use simple image filters to confirm no underlying text shows through.

3) Why “black marker on the printout” can fail
- Simply using a black marker on the original and scanning may leave the text recoverable by adjusting exposure/contrast or from an underlying digital text layer. If you must do it on paper, the safest physical method is to cut out the portion with the SSN, then scan the cut page or photocopy the redacted paper and scan that copy.

4) Remove metadata and hidden data
- PDFs and images can include metadata or hidden text (OCR layers). Use tools to strip metadata (e.g., “Save As” a new file after redaction, or use ExifTool/Preview/your PDF editor’s metadata removal).
- If using a scanner app, disable OCR or ensure the output is an image-only PDF.

5) Secure transmission
- Use a secure channel whenever sending a full SSN:
  - Organization’s secure portal (preferred), SFTP, or encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP).
  - If you must email, consider password-protecting the PDF with a strong password and send the password via a separate channel (phone/SMS).
- Never put the SSN in the email subject line or file name.

6) Other items to check
- Remove or redact additional sensitive identifiers you don’t need to share (birthdate, driver’s license number, account numbers).
- Keep a copy of what you sent and confirmation from the recipient that they received it and will handle it securely.

Quick checklist before you send
- Confirm full SSN is required. If not, send only last 4 (XXX-XX-3456).
- Redact with a proper redaction tool or flatten the image/PDF.
- Verify redaction by trying to select/search and by visual checks.
- Strip metadata.
- Send via a secure channel; do not include SSN in filename or subject.
- If sending full SSN, confirm recipient’s security policy and use encryption.

