Short answer
- Never send a full SSN over an unsecured channel (regular email, SMS). If the form genuinely requires the full SSN, use a secure portal or encrypted transmission.
- If the recipient only needs proof or identity verification, provide just the last 4 digits. Redact the other digits using a true redaction method (not just a black box annotation that can be reversed or left in metadata).

What “true redaction” means (and why it matters)
- Simple black rectangles or annotations can leave the underlying text or searchable OCR in the file. True redaction permanently removes the characters from the file’s text layer and image pixels.
- After redaction, verify by trying to copy/search the document text or view file metadata to ensure the SSN is irrecoverable.

Step-by-step redaction & secure-sending checklist

If you have a paper form:
1. Use a permanent black marker to block the SSN digits you don’t want to share (only when paper is the original and you will keep a copy). Let the ink dry.
2. Scan the document or take a high-quality photo.
3. Before sending, verify the blocked digits are not readable or visible from the back or via light.
4. Prefer to upload the scan via the recipient’s secure portal. If you must email, password-protect and encrypt the file (see below).

If you have a digital file (PDF, image, Word):
1. Determine exactly which digits the recipient needs (ask them if unclear).
2. Use a PDF editor’s built‑in redaction tool (recommended):
   - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools > Redact. Mark text, apply redaction, then save as a new file.
   - Other redaction-capable tools (look for “Apply Redactions” — not just “draw box”).
3. If you don’t have a redaction tool:
   - Convert the file to an image (flatten it) or print to PDF to remove the text layer.
   - Use an image editor (Paint, Preview, etc.) to permanently black out characters.
   - Save as a new PDF/image. Then confirm there is no selectable text.
4. Remove metadata:
   - Export “Sanitized” PDF or use tools (ExifTool, PDF metadata removal, or Acrobat’s Remove Hidden Information).
5. Verify redaction:
   - Try to select or copy the redacted area. Search the PDF for the full SSN — it should not appear.
6. Rename the file without including SSN or other sensitive identifiers in the filename.

How to send securely
- Best: Use the organization’s secure upload portal or encrypted file transfer (SFTP, secure web form).
- If using email:
  - Password-protect the PDF (strong password) and send the password separately (phone or different channel).
  - Use email encryption (S/MIME or the provider’s secure send feature).
- Avoid SMS, regular chat, or an unencrypted email attachment for full SSNs.

Additional practical tips
- Only include the minimum necessary data — often the last 4 digits are sufficient.
- Don’t include other unnecessary identifiers (DOB, account numbers) unless required.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and how (date, method), in case you need to show you redacted appropriately.
- If in doubt, ask the recipient: “Do you need the full SSN or will the last four suffice? Is there a secure upload option?”

