Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer:
- Prefer not to send your full SSN in an email or unencrypted scan. If the requester will accept it, send only the last four digits (e.g., XXX-XX-3456).
- If they require the full SSN, use a secure method (secure portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected PDF delivered separately).

What to redact and when
- If not explicitly required to provide the full number, redact everything except the last four digits (use format: XXX-XX-3456).
- For most identity checks and many forms the last four are sufficient; tax, payroll, background checks, or some government forms often require the full SSN — confirm with the requester.
- Never include the full SSN in an email body or in an unencrypted attachment unless absolutely necessary and you’re using secure transfer.

How to redact safely (best practices)
1. Prefer digital redaction tools over visual cover-ups:
   - Use a PDF redaction feature (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply). This permanently removes the underlying text layer.
   - Do NOT just draw a black box or whiteout over the text in a PDF without using “Apply Redactions” — the original text may remain in the PDF’s text layer and be recoverable.

2. If you must use an image (photo/scan) or don’t have redaction tools:
   - Open the image in an editor, paint the SSN area with a solid color (not white) and save/export as a new flattened image (JPEG/PNG). Then create a new PDF from that image (this eliminates selectable/searchable text).
   - Or print the page, permanently redact with a thick black marker on the printed copy (both sides if applicable), then scan that redacted paper. Verify the scanned file can’t be read or OCR’d.

3. Remove metadata and hidden text:
   - After redaction, flatten the PDF (export or print-to-PDF) so there’s no hidden text layer.
   - Strip EXIF/metadata from images (most image editors or file-properties allow removal; some services do this automatically).
   - Verify by searching/selecting text in the final file — you should not be able to highlight the SSN or find its digits.

4. Secure transmission
   - Use the recipient’s secure portal whenever possible.
   - If sending via email is unavoidable: encrypt the PDF and add a password; send the password over a different channel (SMS or phone). Or use an encrypted email service/secure file transfer service.
   - Don’t put the SSN in the filename.

5. Verification & recordkeeping
   - Ask the recipient to confirm receipt and that the redaction is acceptable.
   - Keep a copy only if necessary and store it securely (encrypted drive). Delete unnecessary copies from cloud or device.

Pitfalls to avoid
- Don’t rely on simply covering text with a white box or highlighting in a PDF — that may leave the underlying text intact.
- Don’t email the full SSN in plain text or as an unencrypted attachment.
- Don’t include the SSN in the file name.

If you want, I can:
- Walk you step‑by‑step to redact a document on your phone, Mac, or Windows computer.
- Create a short email template you can send with the redacted attachment.
Tell me which device and app you’ll use (e.g., Adobe Acrobat, Preview on Mac, Windows, iPhone Photos, Android) and I’ll give precise steps.

