Guidance and steps for sending a scanned document safely
1. Confirm what’s required
 - Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or just the last 4. Many organizations only need the last 4 for ID verification.
 - If the full SSN is required, ask for a secure upload link or encrypted transmission method (secure portal, SFTP, or an encrypted email service).

2. If only the last 4 are needed
 - Redact (hide) every other digit before scanning or sending. Use a standard mask like ***-**-3456 (replace 3456 with your actual last 4).
 - Do not rely on light overlays or just drawing a box in a PDF viewer unless you permanently remove the underlying data (see below).

3. How to redact properly (methods)
 - Physical redaction:
   - Print the document, black out the unwanted digits with a permanent marker (make sure ink fully covers the digits), then scan the marked page. Verify the numbers cannot be read through the ink in the scan.
 - Digital redaction (recommended if available):
   - Use a true redaction tool (e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro’s Redact tool). Redaction removes the underlying text from the file.
   - If using simple annotation (black rectangle) in Preview/Word/PDF editors, “flatten” or “print to PDF” afterwards to burn the annotation into the file and then verify the hidden text is gone.
 - Convert to image:
   - As a fallback, convert the page to a flattened image (JPEG/PNG) and crop or paint over the sensitive numbers, then save as PDF. This prevents selectable text/hidden metadata from revealing the numbers.

4. Verify redaction
 - Try selecting or searching for the redacted digits in the final file; if you can select or find them, the redaction didn’t work.
 - Open the file in a text editor (for PDF) or try copy/paste to check if underlying text remains.

5. Remove metadata and OCR layers
 - If you scanned a document, some PDFs include hidden OCR text or metadata. Use tools or settings to remove metadata and disable/clear OCR text layers, or re-create the document as a flattened image/PDF.
 - On photos, strip EXIF data (phones and many apps can do this or use exiftool).

6. Use secure transmission
 - Use the recipient’s secure upload portal if they have one.
 - If you must email, password-protect the PDF and share the password by a separate channel (text or phone).
 - Avoid sending full SSN over plain email whenever possible.

7. File naming and note
 - Name the file clearly but not with the full SSN (e.g., ID_last4_3456.pdf).
 - In your message, state only what was asked and confirm you redacted other digits.

