Practical guidance

1) Confirm what's required
- Ask the requester whether they actually need the full SSN or just the last four digits for the form. Many organizations accept only the last four plus other identifiers.
- If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure method to submit it (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or in-person/mailed copy).

2) If only the last four are needed
- Mask the rest (e.g., XXX-XX-3456) on the document before scanning or include only the last four on the form.
- Remove or redact any other occurrences of the SSN on the page and on other pages.

3) How to redact safely (don’t just draw a black box unless you flatten the file)
- Best: use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact” feature or other PDF redaction tools). These remove the data from the file rather than hiding it.
- If you use a markup/black box tool, “flatten” or “save as PDF” and then reopen to verify the text underneath is gone. A visible black rectangle can sometimes be removed if it’s just a layer.
- Alternative: print, black out with a permanent marker, then scan the paper copy. This produces a new image without the original text.
- For images or photos from your phone: apply redaction, then export/print-to-PDF to make sure pixels are replaced rather than hidden in layers.

4) Secure transmission options
- Secure upload portal provided by the organization (preferred).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) or password-protected PDF (send the password separately by phone or text).
- Certified mail, tracked courier, or hand-delivery if you must provide the full SSN on paper.
- Avoid sending full SSN via plain email, SMS, or instant messaging.

5) Minimize exposure and keep records
- Only include the SSN on pages where explicitly required; remove it from unrelated documents.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and confirmation that it was received.
- Shred physical copies you no longer need and store digital copies securely (encrypted disk or password manager with secure notes).

6) If you’re unsure or concerned about fraud
- Verify the recipient (call an official number from the organization’s website).
- Ask if you can provide an alternate ID (taxpayer ID, driver’s license, or last 4 + another identifier).

