Short answer
- Only send the full SSN if it's absolutely required. Whenever possible provide only the last four digits (e.g., XXX‑XX‑1234) or use a secure portal/alternate method. If you must send a scan with the SSN, permanently redact everything except the minimum required (often the last four) and send via a secure channel.

What to do (step‑by‑step)

1) Confirm necessity
- Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or if the last four will suffice.
- Ask why they need it, how they will protect it, how long they will keep it, and whether there is a secure upload portal.

2) Verify the recipient
- Confirm the request is legitimate and the email address/domain is correct.
- If this is a company request, call a verified phone number (not the number in the email) to confirm.

3) Redact correctly (don’t just cover text with a black box)
If you must redact a scanned document, use a tool that removes the underlying text/metadata permanently:

Recommended (best)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Use Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions. Then File → Save As (new PDF). Acrobat’s redact tool removes hidden text and metadata.
- PDF editors with “redact” features (Foxit, Nitro, PDFpen): use their redaction function, then save a new file.

If you don’t have a redact tool
- Convert the page to a flat image (no text layer) and then permanently remove the digits:
  - Print the document to PDF/image after blacking out the SSN on the image (e.g., open image in Paint/GIMP, draw a filled black box, export to PDF). Or
  - Take a photo of the printed page with the SSN physically blacked out with a permanent marker (then scan/photograph the result).
- Important: Simply drawing a black box in software on a PDF can leave the underlying (searchable) text intact. Flatten or export to a new PDF/image so the sensitive text is unrecoverable.

Remove metadata
- After redaction, remove metadata and hidden layers. In Acrobat: File → Properties → remove metadata; or re-create a new flattened PDF/image.

4) Masking vs. full redaction
- If only last four are required, mask the rest (XXX‑XX‑1234). Leave only the last four visible.
- If full SSN is required, redact only when possible, or use an alternative secure path (see below).

5) Secure transmission
- Best: Upload to the requester’s secure portal (encrypted, access-controlled).
- If sending by email:
  - Encrypt or password-protect the PDF. Use Acrobat’s password encryption or create a password-protected ZIP (7-zip). Send the password through a separate channel (text or phone).
  - Use enterprise encrypted email or S/MIME if available.
- Alternative: Fax to a known secure fax or deliver in person.

6) Extra protections
- Add a watermark like “For [recipient name] only” if you want to limit reuse (doesn’t stop copying but shows intent).
- Request confirmation that they will delete or securely store the SSN and note retention period.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and when, for your records.

Quick checklist before sending
- Confirm requester legitimacy and necessity.
- Confirm which digits they actually need.
- Redact or mask everything not needed using a proper redaction tool or flattening method.
- Remove metadata.
- Use a secure upload or encrypted/passworded file.
- Send password by a separate channel.
- Ask for confirmation of receipt and secure handling.

