Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the recipient explicitly requires the full SSN and offers a secure submission method, you should redact everything except the last four digits before sending a scanned document. If you must send the full SSN, use a secure upload portal or encrypt the file and share the password separately.

What to do (practical guidance)
- Redact, don’t hide. Use a proper redaction tool so the removed digits are permanently deleted, not just covered by a shape that can be removed.
- Keep only the last 4 digits visible if that’s acceptable to the requester (this is the usual minimum).
- If the full SSN is required, ask for a secure submission method (secure web portal, encrypted upload). Avoid sending full SSN in plain email.

How to redact safely (quick steps)
- Verify policy: Ask the recipient whether they accept last-four only or require the full SSN, and what secure upload options they provide.
- Use a redaction tool (best):
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction (select the digits) → Apply Redactions → Save As a new file. This permanently removes the text.
  - Other dedicated PDF redactors: use the app’s permanent redaction function (not just a black box).
- If you only have simpler tools:
  - Flatten the edit after covering the digits. For example, add a black box over the SSN, then "Print to PDF" or “Export as PDF” to flatten so the black box can’t be removed or copied. (Plain image overlays or annotations can sometimes be undone.)
- For image scans / photos:
  - Open the image in an editor, paint a solid black rectangle over the digits, then save/export as a new image (JPEG/PNG). To be extra safe, convert the image to PDF and then print-to-PDF so nothing remains in editable layers.
- Remove metadata / OCR text:
  - PDFs and images can contain hidden text or metadata. After redacting and saving, search the file (selectable text) to make sure the SSN is not discoverable.
  - Use “Remove personal info” or metadata-strip tools, or export/print-to-PDF to reduce embedded metadata.

If you must send the full SSN
- Use the organization’s secure portal or an encrypted email/file. If that’s not possible:
  - Password-protect the PDF (Acrobat: Protect → Encrypt with Password) or create a password-protected ZIP.
  - Send the file by email and share the password by a different channel (text, phone).
  - Use strong, unique password.

If you already sent the full SSN accidentally
- Contact the recipient immediately, ask them to delete the file and confirm deletion.
- Ask how they secured the file and who has access.
- Consider placing a fraud alert or credit freeze and monitor your credit reports.

Privacy best practices
- Only provide the minimum required information.
