Short answer
- If the recipient only needs the last four digits, send just the last four (or mask the rest: XXX-XX-3456).
- If the form requires the full SSN, do not send it by plain email. Use a secure upload portal, an encrypted/password‑protected file, or another secure method the recipient provides.

Why
- The full SSN is highly sensitive personally identifiable information (PII). Sharing it increases risk of identity theft.
- Many organizations only need the last four for identification; ask the requester whether the full SSN is actually required.

How to redact safely (step-by-step)
1. Confirm requirement
   - Ask the requester whether they accept the last four or need the full SSN and which secure submission method they prefer.

2. Use secure transmission when full SSN is required
   - Preferred: the organization’s secure portal or encrypted upload.
   - Alternatives: password‑protected PDF (send password separately, e.g., by phone or SMS), SFTP, or encrypted email (S/MIME/PGP).

3. Proper digital redaction (don’t just hide)
   - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro “Redact”, or other PDF redaction tools). These permanently remove the underlying data and metadata.
   - If you don’t have a redaction tool: convert the page to an image (scan/export to JPEG/PNG), use an image editor to black out the digits, save as a new file, and then re-create a PDF from that image. That removes underlying text data.
   - After redaction, test that the digits are unrecoverable: try selecting text or copy/paste from the PDF; if you can select the number, the redaction is not safe.

4. Physical redaction
   - Use a heavy permanent marker over the digits, then photocopy the redacted page and scan the copy. Don’t just mark and send the original scan (scanner may pick up traces). Photocopying the marked original makes it safer.

5. Remove metadata
   - For PDFs and images, remove metadata (author, scanner info, hidden form fields). Use the “sanitize” or “remove metadata” option in Acrobat or a metadata-cleaning tool.

6. Keep a secure record
   - Keep a copy only in secure storage (encrypted drive) if needed. Avoid storing the full SSN in email or cloud storage without encryption.

Example masking
- If only last four are needed: XXX-XX-3456 (replace 3456 with the correct last four).
- If you must show proof (e.g., official document) you can send an ID with the full number redacted except the last four.

What to avoid
- Don’t send full SSN in plain, unencrypted email attachments.
- Don’t rely on visually covering digits in an electronic file (e.g., drawing a black box in Word without flattening) — underlying text can remain.
- Don’t post SSNs in chat systems or places that may be indexed/stored insecurely.

