Guidance and practical steps

- Don’t send the full SSN over regular email or as an unprotected photo/PDF unless the recipient explicitly provides a secure method.
- Use only the minimum information needed. If the form asks for the "last 4 digits" or the organization confirms they only need the last four, redact everything except those last four digits.
- Confirm what the recipient actually needs. Ask: “Do you need the full SSN or only the last four? If you need the full SSN, please provide a secure upload link or instructions for encrypted transfer.”

How to redact correctly (important)
- Use a true redaction tool that removes the data from the file (not just visually hides it).
  - Adobe Acrobat Pro: use the Redact tool → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save a new file. Then run “Remove Hidden Information” / “Sanitize” to strip metadata and hidden text.
  - macOS Preview: draw an opaque box over the numbers and then export to PDF; however, Preview can leave selectable text underneath. Prefer dedicated redaction or print-to-PDF after flattening. Better: use a redaction app or Acrobat if available.
  - Windows: use a PDF editor with redaction (e.g., Acrobat, or third-party tools). If you must use Paint/Photos, convert the scan to an image and permanently crop/black out the area, then save as a new PDF/image.
  - Mobile apps: use Adobe Fill & Sign, Adobe Scan, or other apps that explicitly support redaction/sanitization. Verify the app removes OCR text layers.
- After redaction, flatten the document and save as a new file. Verify by trying to select/search the redacted area — it should not reveal the text.
- Remove metadata/hidden data (file properties, OCR text layer, preview images). Tools call this “sanitize” or “remove hidden information.”

Secure ways to send if full SSN is required
- Use the recipient’s secure portal or document upload link (preferred).
- Encrypt the PDF with a strong password and send the password separately (e.g., call or text the recipient the password).
- Use secure file-transfer services (SFTP, encrypted file-share with access control).
- If you must email, use an encrypted email service or attach a password‑protected PDF and avoid including the password in the same email.

Other practical tips
- Keep an unredacted copy in a secure place (encrypted drive) if needed for records, but only share the redacted copy.
- Name files carefully (avoid putting SSN in the filename).
- Verify the recipient’s identity and data-handling practices before sharing sensitive documents.
- If you suspect fraud risk, consider placing a fraud alert/credit freeze and monitor accounts.

