Short answer: only share as much of your SSN as the recipient specifically requires — usually the last 4 digits — and never send the full number over an insecure channel. Also, I noticed the SSN you typed is inconsistent (you wrote "000-00-0123" then "last four: 3456") — please don’t paste your full SSN here. Below are practical steps and options to safely send a scanned document.

Quick checklist
- Confirm whether the form/recipient actually requires the full SSN or just the last 4 digits. If last 4 is enough, redact the rest.
- Use a secure transfer method (secure portal, encrypted email, password-protected file, or secure file transfer).
- Use true redaction (not just a black box or white shape) so the underlying digits are removed from the file and metadata.
- Verify the final file does not contain the SSN by searching the file (Ctrl/Cmd+F) or checking metadata.

When to redact
- If the recipient accepts last 4 digits: redact the first five digits and leave only the last four (e.g., XXX-XX-3456 or ***-**-3456).
- If the form explicitly asks for the full SSN and states a secure submission method, you may need to submit the full number — but prefer secure channels and confirm how they’ll store/delete it.

How to redact properly (recommended methods)
- Best (desktop PDF editor, e.g., Adobe Acrobat Pro):
  1. Use the Redact tool (Tools > Redact). Mark the digits to remove.
  2. Apply redactions and use the “Remove Hidden Information” or “Sanitize Document” option to strip metadata and hidden text.
  3. Save as a new file and search the file for any of the SSN digits to confirm removal.

- If you don’t have Acrobat:
  - Convert the page to a flat image: open the PDF, export or print to a high-quality image (JPEG/PNG) or print to PDF as an image (this flattens text). Then draw a black rectangle over the first 5 digits and save as a new PDF. This prevents the original text from being recoverable.
  - Or open the scan in an image editor (e.g., Paint, Preview on Mac), paint over the digits, save, then convert to PDF.
  - Note: Using a black box in a PDF editor without flattening can leave the original text in the file; flatten or export to an image to be safe.

- Mac Preview tip:
  1. Mark the digits with the black rectangle in Preview.
  2. Export → PDF (or Print → Save as PDF) to flatten. Re-open and search to confirm the digits are gone.

What to avoid
- Don’t just put a white box over text and save — the underlying text may still be in the file.
- Avoid uploading documents with SSNs to random third-party online tools or file-sharing sites unless they’re trusted and encrypted.
- Don’t send the full SSN in plain email unless the email is encrypted (S/MIME/PGP) or the recipient requires it and provides a secure upload portal.

Secure transfer options
- Official secure portal or document upload system (recommended).
- Password-protected PDF (use strong password) and send the password separately (e.g., by phone or a different channel).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) or business email with TLS if both sides support it.
- Secure file transfer services your organization uses (e.g., SharePoint with restricted access, Box with expiration link).

Other safety steps
- Rename the file to avoid including your full SSN or sensitive keywords.
- After sending, delete local copies you don’t need and empty the recycle/trash. If scanned with a shared scanner, check/clear its memory if required by your org.
- Keep a record of who you sent it to and why.

If you want, I can:
