Short answer: yes — redact everything except the portion the form actually requires (often only the last 4 digits). But don’t send the full SSN over an insecure channel; and be careful how you redact so the digits are actually irrecoverable.

A few important notes before instructions
- I noticed you typed a full SSN and also a different “last four.” I won’t repeat those numbers here. Don’t include the full SSN in messages, file names, or non‑secure email.
- If the form only asks for the last 4 digits, supply only the last 4. Ask the requester first if the full SSN is required — they may accept last 4 or another ID.

How to redact safely (paper documents)
1. If you have the paper form:
   - Use a thick black permanent marker to fully cover the unwanted digits on the original.
   - Photocopy the marked original and send the copy (not the unmarked original). The photocopy will not show underlying text.
   - Alternatively, white‑out the digits, let it dry, then photocopy — again, send the copy only.

How to redact safely (digital scans / PDFs / photos)
1. Use a true PDF redaction tool (preferred):
   - Tools like Adobe Acrobat Pro have a Redact feature that removes the underlying text and metadata (not just covers it visually).
   - Use the tool to mark the text to redact, apply/redact, then save a new file.
2. If you don’t have a redaction tool:
   - Convert the page to an image (export/flatten the PDF), then permanently edit the image (paint a black box over digits) and save as a new image or PDF.
   - Make sure you “flatten” or export the file so the black box becomes part of the pixels (not an overlay).
3. With smartphone scanners:
   - Scan, use the markup tool to black out digits, then export/print to PDF (choose “Save as PDF” / “Export”) so the markups are flattened.
   - After exporting, open the exported file and try to copy/paste the redacted area or run a text search to confirm the digits aren’t recoverable.
4. Verify redaction:
   - Try selecting/searching text in the final file; if the digits can be selected or found, the redaction is not safe.
   - Open the file in a text editor to look for residual digits (for searchable PDFs).
   - Check image EXIF/metadata and remove it (many apps or “Save as” options remove metadata).

What to avoid
- Don’t just draw a black box in a PDF viewer that places an overlay — the underlying text may still be recoverable.
- Don’t send the full SSN in an email body or in an unencrypted attachment.
- Don’t include SSN in file names.
- Avoid untrusted online redaction websites unless you trust their security/privacy policies.

Secure ways to send if the full SSN is required
- Use the recipient’s secure portal (preferred).
- Send a password‑protected PDF and share the password separately (by phone or SMS).
- Use an encrypted file transfer service (SFTP, secure share link with access control) or encrypted email (S/MIME or similar).
- As a last resort, provide the SSN by phone or in person.

If you want, I can:
- Walk you through redacting a specific scanned file (tell me whether you’re on Windows, macOS, iPhone, or Android and which apps you have).
- Draft a short message to ask the requester whether last 4 digits are sufficient or whether a secure portal is available.

