Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: yes — you should redact (hide/remove) the full Social Security number whenever possible and only share the minimum required (usually the last 4 digits). If the recipient truly needs the full SSN, send it only by a secure method (secure portal, encrypted email, or in person), not as an unprotected scanned file.

Practical guidance and checklist

1) What to show
- Prefer showing only the last 4 digits (e.g., ***-**-3456). Do not include the full number unless absolutely required.
- Check the form’s instructions: some agencies/employers/IRS portals require the full SSN; if so, use a secure upload mechanism or protected file.

2) Redaction basics (important)
- Use a proper redaction tool that permanently removes the characters from the file (not just visually covers them). Simple black boxes or white rectangles over text can leave the underlying text selectable/searchable and recoverable.
- After redaction, save a new file and verify the SSN cannot be selected or found.

3) How to redact — quick methods by platform
- Adobe Acrobat Pro:
  1. Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction (select the SSN) → Apply Redactions.
  2. Then Tools → Sanitize Document to remove hidden data/metadata.
  3. Save as a new PDF.
- Mac Preview (if you don’t have a redaction tool):
  1. Convert the page with the SSN to an image (File → Export → Format: JPEG/PNG).
  2. Open the image, use the rectangle/markup to cover the SSN, then export back to PDF. This flattens the content.
  3. Remove metadata (see step 4).
  Note: Preview’s black boxes can be non-destructive on the PDF text layer — converting to an image first makes the change permanent.
- Windows (Paint + Print to PDF):
  1. Open the scan, paste into Paint, paint over the SSN, then Print → Microsoft Print to PDF to create a flattened PDF.
- Mobile (iOS/Android):
  - Use a dedicated app with redaction support (Adobe Acrobat Reader mobile, PDF Expert, or Adobe Fill & Sign). Make sure the app’s redact function “applies” or “flattens” the change before saving.
- If you only have a PDF editor that “draws” over text, export/print to PDF (flatten) after covering text to remove underlying text layers.

4) Remove hidden data and metadata
- After redaction, remove metadata and hidden text (authors, EXIF, form fields). In Acrobat: Document → Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize Document. On Windows: File → Properties → Remove Personal Information. For photos, use an EXIF removal tool or phone settings to strip location data.
- Also check the file name — don’t include SSN in the file name.

5) Verify redaction
- Try selecting the area with the cursor; if you can select or copy the SSN, it wasn’t removed.
- Open the PDF in a text editor or run a text search for the digits — they should not appear.
- If you used OCR, run search again after redaction to be sure OCR layer doesn’t contain the SSN.

6) Securely transmit the file
- Best: upload through the recipient’s secure portal (preferred).
- If you must email: password-protect or encrypt the PDF, send the password by a separate channel (call or SMS). Better: use end-to-end encrypted email or a secure file transfer (SFTP, shared secure link with expiration).
- Don’t send an unencrypted file over regular email or public cloud link without protection.

7) If they require the full SSN
- Ask if a secure upload portal is available or request an alternative (phone, in-person).
- If you must send a full SSN scan, use an encrypted channel and minimize copies (delete local copies after confirmation).

8) Keep a record and delete copies
- Keep a note of what you sent and to whom.
- Delete local copies and clear temporary files if you no longer need them.

If you want, I can:
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting your scan on the device you’re using (tell me Windows/Mac/iPhone/Android).
- Draft a short message to the recipient explaining you redacted the SSN and asking for a secure upload link, if needed.

