Short answer: usually you should not send the full SSN in an unsecure scan. Redact everything except the last four digits unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number and they provide a secure method to receive it.

Practical guidance and options

1) Ask the recipient first
- Ask whether they actually need the full SSN or if the last four will suffice.
- Ask what secure transmission method they support (secure portal/upload, encrypted email, or in-person/phone).

2) If you can send only the last four digits
- Redact (permanently remove) the other digits on the scanned document before sending.
- Use a true redaction tool (see #4). Do not rely on a simple black box overlay on a digital PDF that leaves the underlying text intact.

3) If the recipient requires the full SSN
- Prefer secure portal upload or encrypted transfer (SFTP, a vendor portal, DocuSign/secure form) over email.
- If you must email, send a password‑protected PDF (AES‑256) and share the password by a different channel (phone or SMS). Better: use end-to-end encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP).

4) How to redact correctly (recommended methods)
- Use a PDF redaction feature (Adobe Acrobat Pro, or other PDF editors with permanent redaction). This removes the text from the file so it cannot be recovered.
- If you only have a scanner:
  - Print the document, black out the unwanted digits with an opaque marker, then re-scan the paper. This ensures the data is physically removed.
  - Or scan, convert the scan to an image (flatten), and then crop or permanently erase the digits in an image editor and save as a new image/PDF.
- After redaction, confirm by trying to search/copy text from the file — the redacted digits should not be present anywhere.

5) Clean metadata
- Strip document metadata (PDF properties, EXIF data) because it can sometimes contain sensitive text.
- Many PDF tools have a “Remove hidden information” or “Sanitize document” option.

6) Limit exposure and retention
- Don’t put SSNs in the email body.
- Send only what’s needed, to the minimum number of recipients.
- Delete copies from your email/sent folder and from cloud backups when no longer needed (if policies allow).
- Keep a record of who you sent it to and why, in case of audits.

7) If you’re unsure
- Offer to contact the recipient for what they accept, or ask if they can provide a secure upload link.
- If you want, I can draft a short email asking the recipient whether a last‑four is sufficient and what secure upload options they accept.

If you want help drafting that message or step‑by‑step redaction instructions for a specific device or program (Windows, macOS, Adobe Acrobat, Preview, iPhone, etc.), tell me which one and I’ll prepare the exact steps.

