I can help. First — I noticed the full SSN and the “last four” you wrote don’t match; I won’t repeat either number back here. That said, here’s clear, practical guidance so you can safely send a scanned form.

Quick rules
- If the form only needs the last four digits: redact (remove) everything else before sending.
- If the form explicitly requires the full SSN: include it, but only send the scan over a secure channel.
- Never assume email is secure—treat unencrypted email as public.

How to redact safely (best practices)
- Remove the digits permanently, don’t just hide them. Use a proper redaction method that deletes the data rather than hiding it.
- Don’t rely on white-out, folding, or low-opacity shapes that can be reversed or revealed.
- Use a black rectangle or replace the numbers with X’s in an editor, then “flatten” or export to a new PDF/image so the original pixels aren’t recoverable.
- After redaction, verify by opening the file in another viewer and (if possible) zooming/adjusting contrast to make sure the redacted area contains no underlying text or metadata.

How to redact on common platforms
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Use the Redact tool (Tools → Redact). Mark text, then Apply Redactions → Save a new PDF.
- macOS Preview: Draw a filled black rectangle over the digits using Markup, then File → Export as PDF. (To be safer, export and then print to PDF to flatten.)
- Windows (no Acrobat): Open image in Paint, draw a solid black box, save, then Print → Microsoft Print to PDF to create a flattened PDF.
- Mobile (iOS): Use Markup to add a black shape, then Save to Files as PDF. Confirm by reopening the saved file.
- If you scan first: redact digitally after scanning (not by covering on the paper unless you then rescan the redacted paper and confirm nothing shows through).

Removing hidden data and metadata
- Save the redacted document as a new file (don’t overwrite original if you need it).
- Remove metadata: in Acrobat use “Save As” / PDF Optimizer to remove hidden information; in Word/Office use Inspect Document before exporting; on macOS, export/print to a new PDF.
- Change the filename so it does not include SSN.

Secure ways to send the file
- Preferred: upload to the recipient’s secure portal (employer, government agency, medical portal) and submit there.
- Encrypted PDF (password-protect): set a strong password and share the password by a different channel (phone or SMS).
- Encrypted archive: put the file in a 7‑zip or ZIP archive with AES-256 encryption and send the archive; give the password separately.
- Secure file transfer services (SFTP, ShareFile, Dropbox with enforced link password/expiration).
- Avoid unencrypted email attachments. Fax may be acceptable to some organizations but confirm first.
- If you must email, password-protect the file and tell recipient to confirm receipt and deletion of local copies.

Other considerations
- Only send SSN to trusted, verified recipients for legitimate reasons. Ask the requester why they need it and whether last four will suffice.
- Keep a copy of what you sent and when, and who you sent it to.
- If you suspect a breach after sending, contact the recipient immediately and consider freezing credit and monitoring services.

Would you like:
- Step-by-step redaction instructions for a specific device or app (Windows, macOS, iPhone, Android)?
- A short message template to ask the requester whether they need the full SSN or prefer a secure upload method?

Tell me which device or option you want and I’ll walk you through it or draft the message.

