Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — redact the full SSN unless the recipient explicitly requires the full number and you can send it over a secure channel. In most cases organizations only need the last four digits; sending a scanned document that shows the full SSN increases your risk of identity theft.

What to do (quick checklist)
- Confirm requirement: Ask the recipient whether they need the full SSN or only the last four. If they only need the last four, redact the rest.
- Redact correctly (digital files):
  - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark → Apply) or a PDF redaction utility. These remove the text from the file.
  - Don’t rely on simply drawing a black box annotation over text — the underlying text can remain and be recovered.
  - If you edit an image (Photoshop, GIMP), paint over the digits, flatten the image, export to PDF/JPEG, and remove metadata.
  - After redaction, sanitize metadata (Acrobat: Protect → Remove Hidden Information / Sanitize) or use a metadata removal tool.
- Redact correctly (paper documents):
  - Best: remove/cut out the SSN line before scanning, or cover the SSN with an opaque label and then photocopy the covered document so only the copy is scanned.
  - If using marker, place the marker on the original, let it dry, then photocopy — but cutting out or covering + photocopy is safer.
- Secure transmission:
  - Use the recipient’s secure upload portal if they have one.
  - If emailing, password-protect the PDF (256-bit AES if available) and send the password separately (SMS or phone).
  - Prefer encrypted email or secure file-transfer services.
- Check for other occurrences: Make sure SSN isn’t elsewhere on the page (headers/footers, previous pages) and redact all occurrences.
- Keep a secure record: Store any copies you keep in an encrypted location, and delete insecure copies.

Examples of redaction formats
- If they only want last four: XXX-XX-3456 or *****3456 or just 3456 (follow their requested format).
- If you must provide full SSN, confirm a secure method first (portal or encrypted transfer).

Warnings
- I’m not a lawyer — check any legal or institutional policies if this is for a legal/financial/tax purpose.
- Avoid sending full SSN over plain email or unencrypted cloud links.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short email template asking the recipient whether the last four are acceptable (and asking for a secure upload option).
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a PDF on Windows or macOS — tell me which OS and what software you have.

