High-level guidance
- Only share the minimum required. If the form asks only for the last four digits, give only the last four — do not include the full SSN.
- Never send a full SSN over an unsecured channel (plain email, SMS, unencrypted cloud link) unless the recipient specifically requires it and provides a secure method (secure upload portal, encrypted email).
- If you must send a scan that contains an SSN, redact everything that’s not required (physically or digitally) and use secure transfer.

Practical checklist for scanning and sending
1. Confirm requirement
   - Ask the requester: “Do you need the full SSN, or will the last four digits suffice?” If they need the full number, ask them to provide a secure upload link or instructions for encrypted delivery.

2. Redact properly
   - Digital redaction: use a proper redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat’s Redact tool, Preview on macOS with redaction, or a reputable PDF editor). Redaction should remove the underlying text, not just hide it visually.
   - Physical redaction: black marker over the SSN, then photocopy the page (the marker will be permanent on the copy). Don’t just white-out — white areas may be recoverable.
   - After redaction, save/flatten as a new PDF/image so nothing hidden remains in layers or searchable text.

3. Remove metadata
   - Strip metadata/EXIF from the file (many PDF editors and image tools have “remove metadata” or “sanitize document” options).

4. Secure the file for transmission
   - Prefer a secure portal/institution-provided upload. That’s the best option for full SSNs.
   - If you must email: encrypt the file (password-protected PDF with strong password) and send the password by a different channel (phone call or SMS).
   - If you have enterprise tools (S/MIME, PGP), use those.

5. File naming and content
   - Don’t put the SSN in the filename (e.g., avoid “SSN_000-00-0123.pdf”).
   - In the message, only state what you are attaching (e.g., “Attached: redacted ID showing last four digits”) — don’t include the actual SSN in the email body.

6. Retention and disposal
   - Keep copies only as long as necessary. Securely delete local files when no longer needed and shred physical copies.

7. Verify recipient identity
   - Confirm you’re sending to the correct email address or portal. For employers/financial institutions, call a known number from their official website if you’re unsure.

