General guidance and best practices for sending a scanned document that includes an SSN

1) Decide whether the full SSN is actually required
- Ask the recipient what they need and why. Many organizations only require the last 4 digits for verification. If they truly need the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method or alternative secure channel.
- If the full SSN is NOT required, only provide the last 4 digits.

2) How to redact correctly (don’t just cover it visually)
- Use true redaction tools (these remove the underlying text/data), not just a black marker or a white rectangle that leaves the data underneath.
- If you must obscure digits, mask all but the last 4 (e.g., XXX‑XX‑3456 or ***‑**‑3456). Only the last 4 should remain visible when permitted.

3) Tool-specific steps (common options)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro:
  - Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → select the SSN digits → Apply Redactions.
  - Then use File → Save As and use “Sanitize Document” / Remove Hidden Information to remove metadata and hidden text.
- macOS Preview:
  - Preview’s annotations overlay may leave underlying text. Safer options:
    - Export the PDF as an image (File → Export → Format: JPEG/PNG), then open that image and permanently crop or paint over the digits, then save as PDF again.
    - Or use a dedicated PDF redaction app (PDFpen, Adobe) for true redaction.
- Windows (free/cheap PDF editors):
  - Use a PDF editor with a redaction feature (Foxit PhantomPDF, PDF-XChange, Acrobat). Mark redaction, apply, then save.
  - If forced to use a “draw black box” method, print to PDF (Print → Microsoft Print to PDF) after blacking out, then reopen and confirm the text is gone.
- Mobile phones:
  - Avoid only drawing over text with markup. Instead, take a photo of the document after cropping out or covering digits, or use a mobile app that supports secure redaction.
- OCR/searchable PDFs:
  - If the original is searchable text, redaction must remove underlying text; otherwise the SSN can still be extracted. Always verify by trying to select or search for the digits after redaction.

4) Remove identifying information from the file itself
- Don’t include the SSN in the file name (avoid "SSN_000-00-0123.pdf").
- Remove metadata (properties) from the file — many PDF tools have “Sanitize” or “Remove metadata” options.
- Re-check the saved file: try to select text and search for the digits you redacted; if they appear selectable/searchable, redaction failed.

5) Secure transmission
- Use a secure method: the organization’s secure upload portal (HTTPS), encrypted email (S/MIME), an enterprise file transfer service, or an SFTP/secure link.
- If you must email, password-protect the PDF and send the password by a different channel (phone call or text). Use a strong password.
- Confirm receipt and request that the recipient acknowledge how they will store/secure the SSN and how long they’ll retain it.

6) Minimum-disclosure principle & recordkeeping
- Share the minimum needed (prefer last 4 when acceptable).
- Ask how the recipient will store and delete the document. If you emailed it, delete local copies you no longer need, and securely shred/erase if appropriate.

7) Verify redaction worked
- Open the final file on your device and:
  - Try selecting/searching for the redacted digits.
  - If the digits are not selectable or searchable and you used a proper redaction tool, you’re good.
  - Also confirm the filename and metadata contain no sensitive data.

