Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the requester explicitly needs the full SSN, you should redact everything except the last four digits before sending a scanned document. Sending the full SSN over email or in an unprotected file increases your risk of identity theft.

What to do (practical checklist)
- Confirm requirement first
  - Ask the requester whether they truly need the full SSN or whether the last four digits will do (many organizations accept last 4). If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload link or instructions for a protected channel.

- Redact the right things
  - Redact every occurrence of the full SSN (body of the document, headers/footers, form fields, and any OCR/text layer). Keep only the last four digits visible if that’s all that’s required.
  - Do NOT put the SSN in the email subject, filename, or message body.

- How to redact correctly (avoid false “redaction”)
  - For paper documents:
    - Use a black permanent marker to cover the digits, then photocopy or scan the marked copy. Scanning the marked copy ensures the underlying text is not recoverable.
  - For digital scans/PDFs:
    - Use a true redaction tool (Adobe Acrobat Pro has a Redact tool that permanently removes underlying content). Mark and then apply redactions, then save as a new file.
    - If the PDF has an OCR text layer, make sure the redaction removes both the visible pixels and the text layer (apply redaction or flatten/flatten and save).
    - If you only draw a black box or add an overlay without using a redaction feature, the original text may still be recoverable—avoid this.
  - If you must use an online tool, prefer trustworthy services and be aware you’re uploading sensitive data (local tools are safer).

- Secure the file before sending
  - Password-protect or encrypt the PDF and send the password by a separate channel (SMS or phone).
  - Better: use the requester’s secure upload portal or a secure file-transfer service.
  - Remove metadata: check file properties, remove hidden data, and avoid including the SSN in the filename.

- After sending
  - Confirm receipt and ask the recipient to confirm that they received only the allowed information.
  - Keep the unredacted original in a secure place (locked file or encrypted storage).

Risks to note
- Overlays and simple black boxes can be reversed or the text layer recovered.
- Uploading to unknown web sites can expose the data.
- Email is not encrypted by default — attachments travel as clear text unless encrypted.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message to the requester asking whether last four digits are sufficient (I can send it for you).
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting using a specific tool/OS (Windows, macOS, Adobe Acrobat, Preview, or a free local tool).
- Create instructions for password-protecting the PDF and naming it safely.

