Practical guidance and steps

1) Confirm need
- Ask the recipient whether the last four digits are sufficient or if the full SSN is required and why. If they do need the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or instructions for secure transmission.

2) Redaction best practices (make it permanent)
- Use a true redaction tool (not just a black box or whiteout). In PDFs use the built-in Redact/Sanitize feature (e.g., Adobe Acrobat “Redact” then “Apply Redactions”).
- If you don’t have a redaction tool: convert the page to an image, crop out or erase the sensitive area, then save/print to PDF. After that, “flatten” or print-to-PDF so the removed text isn’t recoverable by OCR.
- Verify: after redaction/search, try selecting text or searching for the SSN digits to make sure they’re gone.
- Remove hidden data and metadata (Acrobat “Sanitize” or similar options) so no remnants remain.

3) Secure file handling before sending
- Do not put SSN in the email subject line or filename.
- Use the recipient’s secure portal whenever possible.
- If emailing is the only option: encrypt the file (password-protect the PDF with strong password, AES-256 if available) and send the password separately (text or phone call).
- Consider secure file transfer services (enterprise SFTP, secure upload links) or delivering in-person/by certified mail if very sensitive.
- Avoid sending full SSNs over plain email, SMS, or unencrypted cloud links.

4) Mobile tips
- On iPhone: use Markup to redact then export as PDF and verify; remove image metadata from Photos.
- On Android: use a PDF app with redaction or export edited image and re-save as PDF.
