Two quick safety notes before I give guidance:
- I noticed a mismatch in what you typed (a full SSN that ends differently than the “last four” you wrote). Please don’t paste or repeat your SSN here or in other untrusted chats — I don’t need the actual number to help.
- If you’re about to send a scan that includes an SSN, treat it as highly sensitive and limit exposure.

General rules
- Only share the SSN (full 9 digits) if the recipient explicitly requires it and provides a secure method to receive it. Whenever possible give only the last 4 digits.
- If a form requires the full SSN on paper and you must scan and send it, use secure transmission (secure portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file + separate password delivery).
- If you don’t need to send the full SSN, redact everything except the last four digits (or provide the last four only).

How to redact properly (do NOT just white‑out or crop)
- Use a true redaction tool that removes the underlying data (not just covers it visually). Look for tools labeled “Redact” or “Sanitize.”
- If you don’t have a redaction tool, convert the scan to an image, permanently black out the SSN pixels (not a semitransparent or white box), then save/export that image into a new PDF and “flatten” it so text/OCR is removed.
- After redaction, verify by trying to select/copy the area — the characters should not be selectable or recoverable.
- Remove metadata and hidden data (PDF properties, attached files, OCR text layer). Many PDF editors have a “Remove hidden information” or “Sanitize document” command.

Recommended secure sending methods
- Secure upload portal: Best option if the organization offers it (bank, government, HR portal).
- Encrypted email (S/MIME, PGP) or email encryption service provided by your org.
- Password‑protected PDF (AES‑256 if available) and send the password separately (call or text, not in the same email).
- Secure file transfer (SFTP, secure cloud link with expiration and access controls).
- Avoid sending full SSN in plain email or unsecured messaging apps.

Practical steps by common tools (short)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro: Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply. Then File → Save As new PDF. Use “Remove Hidden Information” and then save.
- Microsoft Print to PDF (Windows): Open image → draw opaque black box over number → Print to PDF. Then inspect to ensure text isn’t selectable and remove metadata.
- macOS Preview: Best to use a proper redaction app; Preview annotations may remain editable. If you must use Preview, after blacking out export to PDF and then print that PDF to a new PDF to flatten — but verify the text can’t be selected.
- Mobile: Use a secure app that supports redaction (Adobe Scan + Acrobat mobile redaction) or take photo → edit with an app that permanently blackens pixels → export to PDF, verify.

Other precautions
- Rename files without including SSN (avoid filenames like SSN_123456789.pdf).
- Limit retention: delete local and cloud copies if not needed, and empty recycle/trash.
- Confirm recipient identity and why they need the SSN; request their data handling/security policy if unsure.
- If you’re unsure whether last four suffice, ask the recipient “Can I provide only the last four digits? If you need the full SSN, what secure method should I use to send it?”

If you tell me:
- whether the form requires the full SSN or just the last four, and
- what device/software you’ll use to scan/send (Windows, Mac, iPhone, Android, Acrobat, Preview, etc.),

I’ll give step‑by‑step instructions for redaction and secure sending tailored to your setup.

