Practical guidance and steps

1) Confirm what’s required
- Check the form or ask the recipient whether they truly need the full SSN or only the last four digits. Many organizations accept only the last four for ID purposes. If they insist on the full SSN, ask for a secure upload portal or an alternative secure method.

2) If only the last four are needed
- Redact (mask) the first five digits so only the last four (3456) remain visible. Example mask: XXX-XX-3456 or 000-00-3456 if they need the area/group preserved — but you should avoid showing the full original pattern unless required. Do not include the full SSN anywhere in the file or message.

3) How to redact correctly (so it’s permanent)
- Use a proper redaction tool (recommended): Adobe Acrobat Pro has a Redact tool that removes the underlying text/data, not just covers it visually. After redaction, use “Remove Hidden Information” or “Sanitize” to remove metadata.
- If you don’t have a redaction tool:
  - Convert the document/page to an image (take a photo or export as PNG/JPG) and crop out or blur the portion with the first five digits; then save that image as a new PDF. This ensures the original text isn’t embedded.
  - If you cover the numbers with a black box in a PDF editor, make sure to “flatten” or export to a new PDF/image and re-open to confirm the original text can’t be selected or searched.
- Do NOT simply draw a black rectangle over text and save without flattening — the underlying text may still be present and extractable.

4) Verify redaction
- Re-open the final file and try to select or search for any part of the SSN digits (e.g., search for “3456” or other digit sequences). If selection/search finds them, redaction is not complete.
- Check document properties/metadata and remove hidden data (remove comments, hidden text, metadata).
- If you converted to an image, ensure OCR text is not re-applied.

5) Secure transmission
- Preferred: use the recipient’s secure upload portal or a secure file transfer service (encrypted, expiring links).
- If you must email: password-protect the PDF with a strong password (AES encryption) and send the password separately (text or phone call).
- Better: use end-to-end encrypted email (S/MIME or PGP) or enterprise secure file-sharing.
- Avoid public Wi‑Fi when uploading/sending sensitive files.

6) After sending
- Keep the minimum number of copies. If you have to keep a copy, store it encrypted and securely. If you printed a copy to redact physically, shred the physical copies when finished.

7) If the recipient requires full SSN
- Request a secure upload link or other secure method.
- If they cannot provide one and you must send, use an encrypted file + separate password transmission as above. Keep a record of why you had to send it and confirm receipt.

