Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: Yes — unless the recipient explicitly needs the full SSN, redact everything except the last four digits. If you must send the full SSN, use a secure method (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or give it by phone) and take steps so the scan can’t be opened or recovered by others.

Do NOT include the full number in plain, unencrypted email. (I won’t repeat your full SSN here; when showing it in files mask the first five digits as XXX-XX-1234 style unless the full number is required.)

Practical guidance and checklist

1) Before you send
- Ask the requester what exactly they need: “Do you need the full SSN or only the last 4 digits?” and “Do you have a secure upload method?”  
- If they only need the last four, only send those (mask the rest: XXX-XX-3456).  
- Verify the recipient’s identity and that the transmission method is secure.

2) If you only need to show the last 4
- Redact or mask the first five digits so the file shows only the last four (e.g., XXX-XX-3456).  
- Also redact any other documents in the scan that include your full SSN (headers, metadata, labels).

3) If you must send the full SSN
- Use a secure channel: the organization’s encrypted upload portal is best. Alternatives: S/MIME or PGP-encrypted email, a password‑protected PDF (password sent over phone or a separate channel), or provide the number by phone.  
- Avoid sending full SSN in plain email or SMS.

4) How to redact safely (common tools)
- Adobe Acrobat Pro (recommended): Tools → Redact → Mark for Redaction → Apply Redactions → Save. Redaction removes underlying text permanently.  
- Adobe Acrobat Reader (free) does not permanently redact without subscription — don’t rely on overlays.  
- Mac Preview: drawing a black box is an overlay and may not remove underlying text. Safer workflow: cover the number with a box, then Export → choose JPEG/PNG (rasterizes) or Print → Save as PDF to flatten. Confirm the underlying text can’t be selected.  
- Windows: open the scanned image in Paint or another image editor, paint over the digits, save as PNG/JPEG, then convert to PDF (Print → Microsoft Print to PDF). That produces a raster image without recoverable text.  
- Mobile: use a reputable app that supports redaction (Adobe Acrobat mobile’s redaction in paid version, or use an image editor to black out and then export as PDF). Avoid unknown scanner apps with poor privacy reputations.  
- Web tools: be cautious — only use trusted services and avoid uploading sensitive docs to unknown third-party sites.

5) Extra steps before sending
- Remove metadata (Document properties) if possible. In PDFs, use “Remove hidden information” or export to image to avoid embedded text/metadata.  
- Don’t include the SSN in the filename (use “ID_doc.pdf” not “SSN_123-45-6789.pdf”).  
- If sending by email, consider password-protecting the PDF and send the password separately (phone or different channel).  
- After sending, delete local copies from devices and empty trash if appropriate, and ask recipient to confirm secure deletion if needed.

6) If you’re unsure
- Ask the organization whether they have a secure upload option or if you can provide the full SSN by phone. Request their instructions in writing.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send to the requester asking whether a secure upload portal exists and whether they need the full SSN or just the last 4.  
- Or, walk you step-by-step through redacting your specific scanned file — tell me what device/tool you’re using (Windows, macOS, iPhone/Android, Acrobat, Preview, etc.).

