Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: only share the minimum required. If the form or recipient only needs the last four digits, redact (remove/hide) the rest of the SSN before scanning or sending. If they truly require the full SSN, send it only by a secure method (secure upload portal, encrypted email, or password‑protected file delivered separately).

Practical guidance and steps

1) Never include the full SSN in:
   - The email subject or filename
   - Unencrypted attachments
   - Public or shared drives
   - Screenshots posted to messaging apps

2) If only the last four digits are required
   - Mask the rest: use a standard format like XXX-XX-3456 (replace with the actual last four).
   - On a scan/image, permanently redact (see redaction notes below). Do not just draw a box over text and leave the underlying text intact.

3) If the full SSN is required
   - Ask whether a secure upload portal or encrypted transmission is available.
   - If you must email it, encrypt the file (see below) and send the password separately (preferably by phone or an alternative channel).

4) How to redact a scanned document properly
   - Best: use a redaction tool that permanently removes the underlying text (Adobe Acrobat Pro has “Redact” + “Sanitize Document”).
   - If you only have Preview (Mac): cover the number with a black box, then Export → PDF and reopen the exported file to verify the text is gone (Preview’s drawing can still leave text selectable — double‑check).
   - If you use an image editor (Paint, GIMP): open the scan, paint over the SSN, flatten/merge layers, then export a new PDF/image. Confirm there is no selectable text.
   - If the PDF has OCR/searchable text, sanitize/remove hidden text/metadata after redaction.
   - Always save as a new file and discard any intermediate files that contain the unredacted SSN.

5) Remove metadata and hidden data
   - Remove document properties, annotations, hidden text and EXIF data (for photos).
   - On macOS Preview or Adobe Acrobat, there are options to remove metadata; there are free online/desktop tools to strip EXIF for photos.

6) Encrypting files
   - Create a password-protected PDF (Adobe, or export from Word with encryption). On macOS: Export → Encrypt. On Windows you can create an encrypted ZIP (7‑Zip with AES‑256) or use a PDF tool.
   - Send the password separately (phone or different messaging channel).

7) Verify the recipient and necessity
   - Confirm who requested the SSN and why. Often organizations will accept alternatives (last four, TIN, employee ID).
   - Confirm they have a secure upload option (many HR, tax, and financial services have secure portals).

8) File naming
   - Avoid putting SSN or other identifiers in filenames. Use neutral names like “Form_2026_YourName.pdf”.

9) Paper copies
   - If you scan a paper form that contains the full SSN and then redact, shred the original paper copy if it’s not needed.

10) If you want, I can:
   - Draft a short message you can send asking the requester to confirm whether the last four is sufficient and whether they have a secure upload portal.
   - Provide step‑by‑step redaction instructions tailored to your OS/tools (Adobe, Preview, Word, phone camera).

