Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer: in almost every case you should redact everything except the last 4 digits before sending a scan — and if the recipient actually needs the full SSN, ask for a secure upload method (not regular email). I won’t repeat the full number you typed; treat it as sensitive.

What to redact
- Remove or black out the full SSN everywhere on the document except the last four digits (if the recipient only needs those).
- Remove any other unnecessary identifiers (birthdate, full account numbers, driver’s license number) unless required.

How to redact safely (paper documents)
1. If you already wrote the SSN on paper:
   - Best: photocopy the page, permanently black out the SSN on the photocopy with an opaque black marker, then scan the photocopy. (Marker on the original can sometimes show through scanning.)
   - Alternative: cover the SSN with opaque tape, photocopy, then scan the copy.
   - Don’t just crop the image — cropping can leave hidden data or be reversed.

How to redact safely (digital documents/PDFs/images)
1. Use a proper redaction tool that removes the underlying text (not just draws a black box):
   - Adobe Acrobat Pro: use the Redact tool and then Apply Redactions, then Save As a new file.
   - PDF editors with a “redact” or “permanently remove” feature work similarly.
2. If you used an overlay (drawing a black rectangle in Preview, Word, or a markup tool), “flatten” the file and verify the text is gone — many overlays leave the underlying text intact and searchable.
3. Confirm removal:
   - Search the PDF for the SSN digits; try copy-paste — if they appear, redaction was not successful.
   - Check document properties/metadata and clear them (Document → Properties → remove personal info or use a “sanitize” feature).
4. For images, edit the pixels (paint black) and save the edited image. Then create a new PDF from that image. Remove EXIF metadata (tools like exiftool or “remove metadata” in image apps).

Transmission: how to send
- Prefer the recipient’s secure portal or an encrypted upload link (ask them).
- If you must send electronically:
  - Don’t send full SSN by plain email. If full SSN is required and the portal isn’t available, send a password-protected PDF (AES encryption) and give the password through a different channel (text or phone).
  - Use encrypted email (S/MIME) only if both sides support it.
  - Avoid third‑party scan apps that upload to their cloud unless you trust their security/policies.

If the recipient actually requires the full SSN
- Ask them for the secure method they prefer (secure portal, encrypted upload, or in-person/mailed copy).
- Confirm exactly which digits they need (sometimes only last 4 are required).

Quick checklist before sending
- Did you redact every instance of the SSN except the last 4 (if appropriate)? Yes/No
- Did you use a permanent redaction method (not just an overlay)? Yes/No
- Did you remove metadata/hidden text? Yes/No
- Are you using a secure transfer method? Yes/No

If you want, I can:
- Draft a short message you can send asking the recipient for a secure upload method.
- Walk you step-by-step through redacting a specific file type (photo, Word, or PDF).
- Verify a file for leftover text if you can safely share it using a secure method you control.

